bravely second end layer is a sweeping  adventure across Celtic myths pirate  legends and science fiction fairy tales  poor writing and forced plot points do  impede the journeys momentum but it's  vivid world and sleek combat are strong  enough to overcome the weight dragging  it down  as a sequel to 2012 bravely default and  lair returns you to Lux and dark a land  of arid deserts and snow-capped  mountains throughout your travels you'll  upgrade for characters engage in  turn-based combat  earn new specialist classes and prevent  the world from crumbling around you the  story serves mainly as a means to pull  you between Lux and darks locales  offering new MacGuffins on a whim as you  navigate class struggles and solve a  variety of political disputes along the  way nobody important we're just looking  for a man named go go ah go get it the  new guy at first and layers variety is  suspect it's hard to tell whether it's a  wild Western a medieval journey or a  dystopian sci-fi drama but after several  hours it's clear the game is using the  gamut of genres to introduce aesthetics  and characters just for spectacles sake  at this point I'm content to let unfold  curious as to what I'll see next and  layers characterization and dialogue  don't reflect the same ambition though  in fact its main character a young  Cavalier named Eugenie Olga is cloying  from the start his catchphrases arrive  during emotional moments his reactions  to plot twists are rarely believable his  optimism and candor remains static  throughout never drawing any real  meaning from his sense of innocence in  naivete few of n layers characters  display much dynamism but U is the  embodiment of the poor writing choices  that drag the adventure down and layer  also breaks the fourth wall several  times throughout often during pivotal  plot moments and it feels forced  every time these meta moments which I  won't spoil here are interesting  concepts on their own but the story  doesn't earn them they cheapen what  little drama the writing already  struggled to impart capture everywhere  can't see everything and what they see I  see  while n layers narrative fails however  its combat succeeds the turn-based title  brings back the brave and default system  established in its predecessor by  defaulting each character enters a  defensive stance and saves up brave  points which allow them to use multiple  actions in one turn do this without  defaulting though and you're useless for  the next several turns and layer  benefits from this risk reward system  much like bravely defaulted there's an  inherent tension when choosing between  careful defense and all-out aggression  but now eponymous bravely second ability  also reprises its role the mechanic  allows you to stop time granting one  character an extra action during that  turn further increasing the possible  ability combinations against your  enemies it's especially useful against  tougher bosses where each turn carries  more weight than it might otherwise and  all of this combat is built on the  franchise's recurring job system jobs  and the array of possibilities  surrounding them are bravely seconds  lifeblood as with the game's varied  locales its class system borrows from a  plethora of genres there's a Templar  there's an exorcist there's even a cat  person there are 30 jobs in total and  each character can equip two at a time  by combining the passive abilities of  one with the active attacks of another  you can create your own disparate  subclasses to further your goals and  layers class system is as much about  experimentation as it is execution  actually acquiring each available job  however leads to problems in n layers  overall structure although many classes  are unlock through the main storyline  some are tied to optional side-quests  that pit to npcs against each other by  choosing sides in the conflict you opt  to fight the opposing character thereby  gaining their job for the remainder of  the game  this is conceptually intriguing but it  often creates situations where the job  you want is at odds with the character  you agree with n layer forces you to  play story above gameplay or in some  cases the other way around without  spoiling too much bravely second does  allow you to retrace your steps in order  to collect every job title in the game's  ladder half but the dissonance of the  earlier side missions halts the momentum  of the globe-trotting plot  on that note the sequels late game hours  are far more enjoyable than those of its  predecessor the pasting is constant  throughout here and you witness  different areas from those of the first  half it's a far cry from the  backtracking and tedium of default  waiting chapters despite end layers new  quests though and the variety of locales  and display here it's worth noting how  familiar the sequel can seem as a whole  for large chunks of the game you're  revisiting the same cities and dungeons  as those from bravely default and the  combat  despite its sleek design doesn't make  any major improvements on the  well-established formula these  complaints don't affect end layers  overall quality but considering the time  the first installment took to beat its  sequel imparts a sense of deja vu more  often than I prefer one can argue it  feels more like an exceptional expansion  than a true sequel but in the end  bravely second transcends the  limitations that its poor writing and  redundant storyline create it's a grand  adventure in every sense of the phrase  whisking you across exotic environments  and satisfying your curiosity every step  of the way it has its restraints but as  the story closes on the plains of Lux  and dark bravely seconds grasp more than  matches its reach we know when the enemy  will strike again  fisherman's ballbuster Magnolia arch
 

